    {
      "id": "adr-samm",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Security practice measured with OWASP SAMM",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 0,
      "concern": "secure-development",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "A team builds this element, and the element is only as secure as the team's habits. The forces: the tools and gates of the other security packs work only while someone in the team knows why they are there and looks after them; a security specialist outside the team is a queue, while one inside every team is time taken from features; maturity that is never measured is assumed, and the assumption is always higher than the truth; a framework adopted all at once turns into paperwork, while targets per practice with a date show what to improve next; a penetration test finds what scanners miss (access across roles, business logic, chained flaws) but covers a few days a year and costs money; training taken once at onboarding teaches the attacks of that year; and buyers, auditors and standards (PCI DSS, US federal buyers) ask for evidence of the process, not only of the product. This pack decides who does the security practices and how their maturity is measured. The tools and gates are decided by the Secure coding, Software supply chain, Threat modelling, Definition of Done and change approval, and Security event logging and detection packs; this pack never decides them again.",
      "decision": "The security practice of the team that builds the element is measured with OWASP SAMM v2. At least every {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}} months the team scores the 15 practices with the SAMM Toolbox (the Excel or Google Sheets workbook from the owaspsamm/core releases; SAMMwise works too) and copies its Scorecard into security/samm/assessment.yaml: the date, and for each practice by its id (G-SM, G-PC, G-EG, D-TA, D-SR, D-SA, I-SB, I-SD, I-DM, V-AA, V-RT, V-ST, O-IM, O-EM, O-OM) the score, the target and the date to reach it; no target is below {{SAMM_FLOOR}}. Targets are set per practice, not one level for all, and someone outside the team (a champion of another team, the central security team) runs the interview. The evidence for a practice is what another pack decided: Threat Assessment the Threat modelling pack, Secure Build the Software supply chain pack, Security Testing and Defect Management the Secure coding pack, Incident Management the Security event logging and detection pack. Security role: {{CHAMPIONS}}. Penetration tests: {{PENTEST}}. Training: {{TRAINING}}.",
      "consequences": "• One score per practice shows where the team stands and what to improve next; the next assessment shows whether it did.\n• A self-assessment flatters: the Toolbox's quality criteria help, but a score is only as honest as the people answering, which is why someone outside the team asks the questions.\n• An assessment takes a day or two of the team's and a facilitator's time each period.\n• SAMM is not a certification: no buyer or regulator takes a SAMM score as proof by itself.",
      "alternatives": "1. NIST SSDF with evidence per practice: what US federal buyers know, but outcomes without levels, so it shows gaps, not progress.\n2. The Microsoft SDL as a checklist: shorter, but done or not done, with no levels.\n3. BSIMM: describes what more than a hundred firms do (BSIMM16), useful to compare against, but it is a study of organisations with a security group, not a target for one team.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "samm"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-ssdf",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Security practice mapped to the NIST SSDF",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 130,
      "concern": "secure-development",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "A team builds this element, and the element is only as secure as the team's habits. The forces: the tools and gates of the other security packs work only while someone in the team knows why they are there and looks after them; a security specialist outside the team is a queue, while one inside every team is time taken from features; maturity that is never measured is assumed, and the assumption is always higher than the truth; a framework adopted all at once turns into paperwork, while targets per practice with a date show what to improve next; a penetration test finds what scanners miss (access across roles, business logic, chained flaws) but covers a few days a year and costs money; training taken once at onboarding teaches the attacks of that year; and buyers, auditors and standards (PCI DSS, US federal buyers) ask for evidence of the process, not only of the product. This pack decides who does the security practices and how their maturity is measured. The tools and gates are decided by the Secure coding, Software supply chain, Threat modelling, Definition of Done and change approval, and Security event logging and detection packs; this pack never decides them again.",
      "decision": "The security practice of the team that builds the element is measured against NIST SP 800-218, the Secure Software Development Framework 1.1. security/ssdf/evidence.yaml maps each of its 19 practices (PO.1 to PO.5, PS.1 to PS.3, PW.1, PW.2, PW.4 to PW.9, RV.1 to RV.3; version 1.1 merged PW.3 into PW.4) to the evidence that the team follows it, a repository path or a link, and is reviewed at least every {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}} months. Most evidence is a decision of another pack: PW.1 and PW.2 the Threat modelling pack, PS.1 to PS.3 and PW.4 the Software supply chain pack, PW.5, PW.7, PW.8 and RV.1 to RV.3 the Secure coding pack; PO.2 is this pack's security role and training. SSDF 1.2 (SP 800-218 Rev. 1, an initial public draft of December 2025) adds the practices PO.6 and PW.10; they join the map when it is final. When a US federal agency asks for the CISA Secure Software Development Attestation Form, it is filled from the map and signed by the chief executive or a designee who can bind the company; OMB M-26-05 (January 2026) rescinded the memoranda that made the form mandatory, so each agency now decides by its own risk-based policy whether to ask for it. Security role: {{CHAMPIONS}}. Penetration tests: {{PENTEST}}. Training: {{TRAINING}}.",
      "consequences": "• Speaks the language of US federal buyers and of many enterprise questionnaires; the map answers them in hours instead of weeks.\n• The SSDF says which outcome to reach, not how well: the map shows gaps, not progress, so improvement needs goals of its own.\n• The map goes stale as workflows are renamed; the check catches a missing repository path, but a link outside the repository is checked only by the reviewer.\n• Version 1.2 will add practices; the map grows when it is final.",
      "alternatives": "1. OWASP SAMM with targets: levels and progress, but buyers asking for the SSDF need a mapping on top.\n2. The Microsoft SDL as a checklist: shorter, and less known to buyers.\n3. The attestation form alone, without a map: a signature with nothing under it.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "ssdf"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-sdl",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "The Microsoft SDL as the team's checklist",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 260,
      "concern": "secure-development",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "A team builds this element, and the element is only as secure as the team's habits. The forces: the tools and gates of the other security packs work only while someone in the team knows why they are there and looks after them; a security specialist outside the team is a queue, while one inside every team is time taken from features; maturity that is never measured is assumed, and the assumption is always higher than the truth; a framework adopted all at once turns into paperwork, while targets per practice with a date show what to improve next; a penetration test finds what scanners miss (access across roles, business logic, chained flaws) but covers a few days a year and costs money; training taken once at onboarding teaches the attacks of that year; and buyers, auditors and standards (PCI DSS, US federal buyers) ask for evidence of the process, not only of the product. This pack decides who does the security practices and how their maturity is measured. The tools and gates are decided by the Secure coding, Software supply chain, Threat modelling, Definition of Done and change approval, and Security event logging and detection packs; this pack never decides them again.",
      "decision": "The security practice of the team that builds the element follows the Microsoft Security Development Lifecycle as a checklist. security/sdl/checklist.yaml lists the ten practices on Microsoft's SDL page (security standards, metrics and governance; proven security features, languages and frameworks; security design review and threat modelling; cryptography standards; the software supply chain; the engineering environment; security testing; operational platform security; security monitoring and response; security training), each marked done, partial or not-applicable with its evidence, what is missing or a reason, and is reviewed at least every {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}} months against the page as it stands. Each item points at the pack that decides it: threat modelling the Threat modelling pack, cryptography the Encryption in transit and at rest pack, the supply chain the Software supply chain pack, security testing the Secure coding pack, monitoring and response the Security event logging and detection pack. Security role: {{CHAMPIONS}}. Penetration tests: {{PENTEST}}. Training: {{TRAINING}}.",
      "consequences": "• Ten items a small team can keep in mind and review in an hour.\n• Done or not done hides how well: partial with a note is the honest middle, and there are no levels to show progress.\n• Microsoft changes the SDL page without version numbers; the review compares the checklist with the page.\n• Few buyers ask for the SDL by name; a questionnaire still needs its own mapping.",
      "alternatives": "1. OWASP SAMM: levels and targets per practice, for a day or two per assessment.\n2. NIST SSDF: what US federal buyers ask for, 19 practices with evidence.\n3. No framework: nobody can say which practices are missing.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "sdl"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-champions",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "A security champion in each team",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 390,
      "concern": "secure-development",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "A team builds this element, and the element is only as secure as the team's habits. The forces: the tools and gates of the other security packs work only while someone in the team knows why they are there and looks after them; a security specialist outside the team is a queue, while one inside every team is time taken from features; maturity that is never measured is assumed, and the assumption is always higher than the truth; a framework adopted all at once turns into paperwork, while targets per practice with a date show what to improve next; a penetration test finds what scanners miss (access across roles, business logic, chained flaws) but covers a few days a year and costs money; training taken once at onboarding teaches the attacks of that year; and buyers, auditors and standards (PCI DSS, US federal buyers) ask for evidence of the process, not only of the product. This pack decides who does the security practices and how their maturity is measured. The tools and gates are decided by the Secure coding, Software supply chain, Threat modelling, Definition of Done and change approval, and Security event logging and detection packs; this pack never decides them again.",
      "decision": "Each team that owns code of the element names one developer as its security champion in security/champions.yaml, with at least {{CHAMPION_HOURS}} hours a week set aside in the team's plan. The champions form the GitHub team {{SECURITY_TEAM}}, meet monthly as a group, get training beyond the course every engineer takes, and have the role in their goals. A champion facilitates the team's threat modelling (its method and timing are the Threat modelling pack's), keeps the framework record with the engineering lead, and gives the security review: a pull request with the security-review label, set by its author or a reviewer when the Definition of Done or the threat model asks for a security review, is merged only after a champion who is not its author approves it. The security-review check enforces this; it is added to the required checks of main in the ruleset the Code review and branching pack keeps. A champion who leaves is replaced before the team's next labelled pull request.",
      "consequences": "• Security questions are answered inside the team, by someone who knows the code, without a queue.\n• {{CHAMPION_HOURS}} hours a week per team are not spent on features; without them the role exists only on paper.\n• A champion is not a specialist: hard questions still need outside help, budgeted separately.\n• The role rests on one person per team; a departure leaves a gap, which the check reports.",
      "alternatives": "1. A central application security team: depth and consistent reviews, but a queue and distance from the code.\n2. No dedicated role: nothing to staff, and nobody accountable when a gate is switched off.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "CHAMPIONS": [
          "champions"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-appsec",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "A central application security team reviews",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 520,
      "concern": "secure-development",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "A team builds this element, and the element is only as secure as the team's habits. The forces: the tools and gates of the other security packs work only while someone in the team knows why they are there and looks after them; a security specialist outside the team is a queue, while one inside every team is time taken from features; maturity that is never measured is assumed, and the assumption is always higher than the truth; a framework adopted all at once turns into paperwork, while targets per practice with a date show what to improve next; a penetration test finds what scanners miss (access across roles, business logic, chained flaws) but covers a few days a year and costs money; training taken once at onboarding teaches the attacks of that year; and buyers, auditors and standards (PCI DSS, US federal buyers) ask for evidence of the process, not only of the product. This pack decides who does the security practices and how their maturity is measured. The tools and gates are decided by the Secure coding, Software supply chain, Threat modelling, Definition of Done and change approval, and Security event logging and detection packs; this pack never decides them again.",
      "decision": "A central application security team, the GitHub team {{SECURITY_TEAM}}, gives the security review: a pull request with the security-review label, set by its author or a reviewer when the Definition of Done or the threat model asks for a security review, is merged only after a member of that team who is not its author approves it, and the team answers within {{APPSEC_REVIEW_DAYS}} days of the label; a daily job reports pull requests waiting longer. The security-review check is added to the required checks of main in the ruleset the Code review and branching pack keeps. The central team also facilitates threat modelling with the developers (the method is the Threat modelling pack's), runs the framework assessment with the engineering lead, and scopes the penetration tests.",
      "consequences": "• Reviews by specialists, the same across teams.\n• A queue: a change may wait {{APPSEC_REVIEW_DAYS}} days, and teams learn to label less; watch how many reviews are asked for.\n• Knowledge stays with the central team; developers learn only what the reviews teach them.\n• Needs a central team with enough people to keep the deadline.",
      "alternatives": "1. A security champion in each team: no queue, less depth.\n2. Champions with the central team behind them for the hard cases: often the next step, recorded by superseding this decision.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "CHAMPIONS": [
          "appsec"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-no-security-role",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "No dedicated security role",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 650,
      "concern": "secure-development",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "A team builds this element, and the element is only as secure as the team's habits. The forces: the tools and gates of the other security packs work only while someone in the team knows why they are there and looks after them; a security specialist outside the team is a queue, while one inside every team is time taken from features; maturity that is never measured is assumed, and the assumption is always higher than the truth; a framework adopted all at once turns into paperwork, while targets per practice with a date show what to improve next; a penetration test finds what scanners miss (access across roles, business logic, chained flaws) but covers a few days a year and costs money; training taken once at onboarding teaches the attacks of that year; and buyers, auditors and standards (PCI DSS, US federal buyers) ask for evidence of the process, not only of the product. This pack decides who does the security practices and how their maturity is measured. The tools and gates are decided by the Secure coding, Software supply chain, Threat modelling, Definition of Done and change approval, and Security event logging and detection packs; this pack never decides them again.",
      "decision": "Nobody in or around the team has security as a role. The security questions of a change are part of the peer review the Code review and branching pack requires, the engineering lead keeps the framework record, and no change waits for a security specialist. This holds while one small team builds the element for internal use; the first external customer, audit or second team supersedes this decision.",
      "consequences": "• Nothing to staff, no hours set aside, no queue.\n• Nobody is accountable when a security gate is switched off or a finding ages, and the framework's people practices (SAMM Education and Guidance, SSDF PO.2, the SDL's roles) score low.\n• The depth of a security review is whatever the reviewer happens to know.",
      "alternatives": "1. A security champion in each team: a few hours a week, one person accountable.\n2. A central application security team: depth, at the cost of a queue.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "CHAMPIONS": [
          "none"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-pentest",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "External penetration tests",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 780,
      "concern": "secure-development",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "A team builds this element, and the element is only as secure as the team's habits. The forces: the tools and gates of the other security packs work only while someone in the team knows why they are there and looks after them; a security specialist outside the team is a queue, while one inside every team is time taken from features; maturity that is never measured is assumed, and the assumption is always higher than the truth; a framework adopted all at once turns into paperwork, while targets per practice with a date show what to improve next; a penetration test finds what scanners miss (access across roles, business logic, chained flaws) but covers a few days a year and costs money; training taken once at onboarding teaches the attacks of that year; and buyers, auditors and standards (PCI DSS, US federal buyers) ask for evidence of the process, not only of the product. This pack decides who does the security practices and how their maturity is measured. The tools and gates are decided by the Secure coding, Software supply chain, Threat modelling, Definition of Done and change approval, and Security event logging and detection packs; this pack never decides them again.",
      "decision": "An external penetration testing firm attacks the element: {{PENTEST}}. Each test follows the OWASP Web Security Testing Guide, signs in as each role, covers the element's APIs and the business logic of its main journeys, and ends with a report and a retest of high and critical findings. Every test is recorded in security/pentests.yaml (date, tester, scope, the release it was for, a link to the report in a restricted store, never the report itself, and the date its findings were filed); its findings become findings of the Secure coding pack, with that pack's deadlines, within {{PENTEST_TRIAGE_DAYS}} days of the report. A major release is one whose tag matches {{MAJOR_TAGS}}.",
      "consequences": "• Finds what scanners miss: access across roles, business logic, chained flaws.\n• Each test costs money and one to three weeks with the retest; it is budgeted with the releases.\n• A test is a snapshot: code merged the week after is untested until the next one, which is why the Secure coding pack's scanners keep running.\n• Before a major release the test is on the release's critical path; it is booked weeks ahead.",
      "alternatives": "1. No external test, with dynamic scanning and a bug bounty instead: cheaper, but access control and business logic go untested by anyone thinking like an attacker.\n2. An internal red team: knows the system, shares its builders' blind spots, and few organisations have one.\n3. Penetration testing as a subscription: shorter cycles, worth it with many releases a year.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "PENTEST": [
          "yearly",
          "major"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-no-pentest",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "No penetration test, with a replacement recorded",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 910,
      "concern": "secure-development",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "A team builds this element, and the element is only as secure as the team's habits. The forces: the tools and gates of the other security packs work only while someone in the team knows why they are there and looks after them; a security specialist outside the team is a queue, while one inside every team is time taken from features; maturity that is never measured is assumed, and the assumption is always higher than the truth; a framework adopted all at once turns into paperwork, while targets per practice with a date show what to improve next; a penetration test finds what scanners miss (access across roles, business logic, chained flaws) but covers a few days a year and costs money; training taken once at onboarding teaches the attacks of that year; and buyers, auditors and standards (PCI DSS, US federal buyers) ask for evidence of the process, not only of the product. This pack decides who does the security practices and how their maturity is measured. The tools and gates are decided by the Secure coding, Software supply chain, Threat modelling, Definition of Done and change approval, and Security event logging and detection packs; this pack never decides them again.",
      "decision": "The element has no external penetration test. security/pentests.yaml records why and what replaces it (the dynamic testing decided in the Secure coding pack, a bug bounty programme, an internal red team exercise), and the record is reviewed at least every {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}} months. Payment data, the first external customer, or a contract or standard that asks for a penetration test (PCI DSS Requirement 11.4 does) supersedes this decision.",
      "consequences": "• No test to buy, schedule or wait for.\n• Access control across roles and business logic are tested only by the team that wrote them.\n• The framework's testing practice (SAMM Security Testing, SSDF PW.8, SDL security testing) scores lower, and the record says so.",
      "alternatives": "1. An external test every year and before each major release: what PCI DSS asks for.\n2. An external test before each major release only: one test per release, nothing between.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "PENTEST": [
          "none"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-samm",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "SAMM assessment current and on target",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 0,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "The assessment is at most {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}} months old; all 15 practices have a score from 0 to 3 and a target from {{SAMM_FLOOR}} to 3; 0 practices below target after their date",
      "description": "A scheduled job, and every pull request that changes security/samm/, reads the assessment copied from the SAMM Toolbox's Scorecard and fails on a stale date, a missing practice, a target below the floor, or a practice below its target after its date. The file is a format of this pack: the Toolbox is a workbook with no machine-readable export, so the Scorecard's practice scores are copied into it after each assessment.\n\nTool: Node.js with js-yaml (scripts/security/samm-check.mjs) in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```js\n// scripts/security/samm-check.mjs; security/samm/assessment.yaml looks like:\n//   assessed: 2026-09-15\n//   practices: { G-SM: { score: 1.5, target: 2, by: 2027-06-30 }, G-PC: { ... }, ... }\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst PRACTICES = ['G-SM', 'G-PC', 'G-EG', 'D-TA', 'D-SR', 'D-SA', 'I-SB', 'I-SD', 'I-DM', 'V-AA', 'V-RT', 'V-ST', 'O-IM', 'O-EM', 'O-OM']\nconst MONTHS = {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}}, FLOOR = {{SAMM_FLOOR}}\nconst a = yaml.load(readFileSync('security/samm/assessment.yaml', 'utf8'), { schema: yaml.JSON_SCHEMA })\nconst oldest = new Date(); oldest.setMonth(oldest.getMonth() - MONTHS)\nconst errors = []\nif (!(new Date(a.assessed) >= oldest)) errors.push(`assessment of ${a.assessed} is older than ${MONTHS} months`)\nfor (const id of PRACTICES) {\n  const p = a.practices?.[id]\n  if (!p) { errors.push(`${id}: no score`); continue }\n  if (!(p.score >= 0 && p.score <= 3)) errors.push(`${id}: score ${p.score} is not from 0 to 3`)\n  if (!(p.target >= FLOOR && p.target <= 3)) errors.push(`${id}: target ${p.target} is not from ${FLOOR} to 3`)\n  if (p.score < p.target && !(new Date(p.by) > new Date())) errors.push(`${id}: ${p.score} is below its target ${p.target}, due ${p.by ?? 'on no date'}`)\n}\nif (errors.length) { console.error(errors.join('\\n')); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "samm"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-ssdf",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "SSDF evidence map complete",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 130,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "All 19 SSDF 1.1 practice ids have at least one piece of evidence or a reason; 0 repository paths in the map missing; the map reviewed within {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}} months",
      "description": "A scheduled job, and every pull request, reads security/ssdf/evidence.yaml and fails on a missing practice id, a practice with neither evidence nor a reason, a repository path that no longer exists (a renamed workflow), or a stale review date. Links outside the repository must be https links; whether they still show what they claim is the reviewer's part. When SSDF 1.2 is final, PO.6 and PW.10 are added to the list.\n\nTool: Node.js with js-yaml (scripts/security/ssdf-check.mjs) in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```js\n// scripts/security/ssdf-check.mjs; security/ssdf/evidence.yaml looks like:\n//   reviewed: 2026-09-20\n//   practices: { PO.1: [docs/security/requirements.md], PO.2: [security/champions.yaml], PW.6: { na: \"interpreted code, no compiler\" } }\nimport { existsSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst IDS = ['PO.1', 'PO.2', 'PO.3', 'PO.4', 'PO.5', 'PS.1', 'PS.2', 'PS.3', 'PW.1', 'PW.2', 'PW.4', 'PW.5', 'PW.6', 'PW.7', 'PW.8', 'PW.9', 'RV.1', 'RV.2', 'RV.3']\nconst MONTHS = {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}}\nconst map = yaml.load(readFileSync('security/ssdf/evidence.yaml', 'utf8'), { schema: yaml.JSON_SCHEMA })\nconst oldest = new Date(); oldest.setMonth(oldest.getMonth() - MONTHS)\nconst errors = []\nif (!(new Date(map.reviewed) >= oldest)) errors.push(`evidence map last reviewed ${map.reviewed}, more than ${MONTHS} months ago`)\nfor (const id of IDS) {\n  const e = map.practices?.[id]\n  if (e?.na) continue\n  if (!Array.isArray(e) || !e.length) { errors.push(`${id}: no evidence and no reason`); continue }\n  for (const link of e) {\n    if (/^https?:/.test(link) ? !link.startsWith('https://') : !existsSync(link)) errors.push(`${id}: ${link} does not exist or is not https`)\n  }\n}\nif (errors.length) { console.error(errors.join('\\n')); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "ssdf"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-sdl",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "SDL checklist complete and reviewed",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 260,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "All 10 SDL practices present; each done or partial item has evidence, each partial item says what is missing, each not-applicable item a reason; reviewed within {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}} months",
      "description": "A scheduled job, and every pull request, reads security/sdl/checklist.yaml, whose keys are this pack's short names for the ten practices on Microsoft's SDL page in the page's order, and fails on a missing practice, an unsupported status or a stale review date.\n\nTool: Node.js with js-yaml (scripts/security/sdl-check.mjs) in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```js\n// scripts/security/sdl-check.mjs; security/sdl/checklist.yaml looks like:\n//   reviewed: 2026-09-20\n//   practices: { threat-modeling: { status: partial, evidence: [docs/threat-model.md], missing: \"the admin UI\" }, ... }\nimport { existsSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst PRACTICES = ['standards-governance', 'proven-features', 'threat-modeling', 'cryptography', 'supply-chain',\n  'engineering-environment', 'security-testing', 'platform-security', 'monitoring-response', 'training']\nconst MONTHS = {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}}\nconst list = yaml.load(readFileSync('security/sdl/checklist.yaml', 'utf8'), { schema: yaml.JSON_SCHEMA })\nconst oldest = new Date(); oldest.setMonth(oldest.getMonth() - MONTHS)\nconst errors = []\nif (!(new Date(list.reviewed) >= oldest)) errors.push(`checklist last reviewed ${list.reviewed}`)\nfor (const id of PRACTICES) {\n  const p = list.practices?.[id] ?? {}\n  if (p.status === 'not-applicable') { if (!p.reason) errors.push(`${id}: not applicable without a reason`); continue }\n  if (!['done', 'partial'].includes(p.status)) { errors.push(`${id}: status is ${p.status}`); continue }\n  if (!p.evidence?.length || p.evidence.some((e) => !e.startsWith('https://') && !existsSync(e))) errors.push(`${id}: evidence missing`)\n  if (p.status === 'partial' && !p.missing) errors.push(`${id}: partial without saying what is missing`)\n}\nif (errors.length) { console.error(errors.join('\\n')); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "sdl"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-champions",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "A champion named for each team",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 390,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 teams in CODEOWNERS without a champion in security/champions.yaml; every champion a member of their team and of {{SECURITY_TEAM}}, with at least {{CHAMPION_HOURS}} hours a week",
      "description": "A weekly job reads security/champions.yaml and the teams named in .github/CODEOWNERS, asks GitHub for the members of each team and of the champions' team, and fails naming a team without a champion, a champion who has left their team or the champions' team, or one with fewer hours than decided. With collective ownership (no CODEOWNERS), the teams listed in the file are checked.\n\nTool: Node.js with js-yaml and the GitHub CLI (gh api orgs/{org}/teams/{team_slug}/members, a token with read access to the organisation's members)\n\n```js\n// scripts/security/champions-check.mjs; security/champions.yaml: { teams: { order-team: { champion: alice, hours: 4 } } }\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process'\nimport { existsSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst ORG = process.env.GITHUB_REPOSITORY_OWNER, HOURS = {{CHAMPION_HOURS}}\nconst { teams = {} } = yaml.load(readFileSync('security/champions.yaml', 'utf8'), { schema: yaml.JSON_SCHEMA })\nconst members = (team) => JSON.parse(execFileSync('gh', ['api', '--paginate', '--slurp', `orgs/${ORG}/teams/${team}/members`], { encoding: 'utf8' })).flat().map((m) => m.login)\nconst owners = existsSync('.github/CODEOWNERS')\n  ? [...readFileSync('.github/CODEOWNERS', 'utf8').matchAll(new RegExp(`@${ORG}/([\\\\w.-]+)`, 'gi'))].map((m) => m[1]) : []\nconst errors = []\nfor (const team of new Set(owners)) if (!teams[team]) errors.push(`${team} owns code but has no champion`)\nconst reviewers = members('{{SECURITY_TEAM}}')\nfor (const [team, c] of Object.entries(teams)) {\n  if (!c?.champion) { errors.push(`${team}: no champion named`); continue }\n  if (!members(team).includes(c.champion)) errors.push(`${team}: ${c.champion} is not in the team`)\n  if (!reviewers.includes(c.champion)) errors.push(`${team}: ${c.champion} is not in {{SECURITY_TEAM}}`)\n  if (!(c.hours >= HOURS)) errors.push(`${team}: ${c.hours ?? 0} hours a week, fewer than ${HOURS}`)\n}\nif (errors.length) { console.error(errors.join('\\n')); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "CHAMPIONS": [
          "champions"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-security-review",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Labelled changes need a security approval",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 520,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 pull requests with the security-review label merged without an approval, still standing, from a member of {{SECURITY_TEAM}} who is not the author",
      "description": "A workflow runs on every change to a pull request's labels, commits and reviews; when the security-review label is set, it fails unless the latest review of some member of {{SECURITY_TEAM}} other than the author is an approval. The job is a required check of main, in the ruleset the Code review and branching pack keeps. GITHUB_TOKEN cannot list team members, so the workflow uses a token with read access to the organisation's members (a GitHub App installation token or a fine-grained token) stored as ORG_READ_TOKEN.\n\nTool: GitHub Actions with the GitHub CLI (pulls/{pull_number}/reviews and orgs/{org}/teams/{team_slug}/members)\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/security-review.yml\nname: security-review\non:\n  pull_request:\n    types: [opened, reopened, synchronize, labeled, unlabeled]\n  pull_request_review:\n    types: [submitted, dismissed]\njobs:\n  security-review:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: Approved by a security reviewer\n        if: contains(github.event.pull_request.labels.*.name, 'security-review')\n        env:\n          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.ORG_READ_TOKEN }}\n          PR: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}\n          AUTHOR: ${{ github.event.pull_request.user.login }}\n        run: |\n          team=$(gh api --paginate \"orgs/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY_OWNER/teams/{{SECURITY_TEAM}}/members\" --jq '.[].login')\n          approvers=$(gh api --paginate \"repos/$GITHUB_REPOSITORY/pulls/$PR/reviews\" --jq '.[] | select(.state != \"COMMENTED\") | \"\\(.user.login) \\(.state)\"' \\\n            | awk '{ last[$1] = $2 } END { for (u in last) if (last[u] == \"APPROVED\") print u }')\n          for u in $approvers; do\n            if [ \"$u\" != \"$AUTHOR\" ] && grep -qxF \"$u\" <<< \"$team\"; then exit 0; fi\n          done\n          echo \"the security-review label needs an approval from {{SECURITY_TEAM}}\"; exit 1\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "CHAMPIONS": [
          "champions",
          "appsec"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-one-team",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "One team owns the code",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 650,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "At most 1 distinct team named in .github/CODEOWNERS",
      "description": "A check on every pull request counts the distinct teams that .github/CODEOWNERS names (written @org/team) and fails above one, so that a second team cannot own code while the decision that nobody has security as a role stands. Without a CODEOWNERS file (collective ownership) it passes.\n\nTool: grep in a GitHub Actions step\n\n```bash\n# a step of the pull request workflow\nset -euo pipefail\n[ -f .github/CODEOWNERS ] || exit 0\nteams=$(grep -v '^[[:space:]]*#' .github/CODEOWNERS | grep -oE '@[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+' | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' | sort -u)\ncount=$(printf '%s' \"$teams\" | grep -c . || true)\nif [ \"$count\" -gt 1 ]; then\n  echo \"CODEOWNERS names $count teams; supersede the decision that nobody has security as a role:\"\n  echo \"$teams\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "CHAMPIONS": [
          "none"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-appsec-wait",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Security reviews given in time",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 780,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 open pull requests labelled security-review for more than {{APPSEC_REVIEW_DAYS}} days without an approval from {{SECURITY_TEAM}}",
      "description": "A daily job lists the open pull requests with the security-review label, skips those a member of {{SECURITY_TEAM}} has approved, finds when the label was last added from the issue's events, and fails naming each one that has waited longer than the deadline, so the wait shows up before the team learns to avoid the label.\n\nTool: GitHub CLI (gh pr list, gh pr view --json latestReviews, gh api issues/{issue_number}/events) in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```bash\n# scheduled daily, with GH_TOKEN able to read the organisation's teams\nset -euo pipefail\nteam=$(gh api --paginate \"orgs/{owner}/teams/{{SECURITY_TEAM}}/members\" --jq '.[].login')\nlate=0\nfor pr in $(gh pr list --label security-review --state open --json number --jq '.[].number'); do\n  approved=$(gh pr view \"$pr\" --json latestReviews --jq '.latestReviews[] | select(.state == \"APPROVED\") | .author.login' \\\n    | grep -cxF -f <(printf '%s\\n' \"$team\") || true)\n  [ \"$approved\" -gt 0 ] && continue\n  since=$(gh api --paginate \"repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/$pr/events\" \\\n    --jq '.[] | select(.event == \"labeled\" and .label.name == \"security-review\") | .created_at' | tail -1)\n  days=$(( ( $(date +%s) - $(date -d \"$since\" +%s) ) / 86400 ))\n  if [ \"$days\" -gt {{APPSEC_REVIEW_DAYS}} ]; then echo \"#$pr has waited $days days for a security review\"; late=1; fi\ndone\nexit \"$late\"\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "CHAMPIONS": [
          "appsec"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-pentest-age",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Penetration test not older than the limit",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 910,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "The newest penetration test in security/pentests.yaml is at most {{PENTEST_MONTHS}} months old",
      "description": "A scheduled job reads the dates of the tests recorded in security/pentests.yaml and fails when the newest is older than the limit, a month before which the next test should already be booked.\n\nTool: Node.js with js-yaml (scripts/security/pentest-age.mjs) in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```js\n// scripts/security/pentest-age.mjs\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst MONTHS = {{PENTEST_MONTHS}}\nconst { tests = [] } = yaml.load(readFileSync('security/pentests.yaml', 'utf8'), { schema: yaml.JSON_SCHEMA })\nconst newest = tests.map((t) => new Date(t.date)).sort((a, b) => b - a)[0]\nconst oldest = new Date(); oldest.setMonth(oldest.getMonth() - MONTHS)\nif (!(newest >= oldest)) {\n  console.error(`no penetration test in the last ${MONTHS} months (newest: ${newest?.toISOString().slice(0, 10) ?? 'none'})`)\n  process.exit(1)\n}\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "PENTEST": [
          "yearly"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-pentest-record",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Tests recorded, findings filed, major releases tested",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1040,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "Every recorded test names its tester, scope and report and had its findings filed within {{PENTEST_TRIAGE_DAYS}} days; every tag matching {{MAJOR_TAGS}} since the decision has a test for it dated no later than the tag",
      "description": "A job on every pushed tag, and weekly, reads security/pentests.yaml and the repository's tags, and fails on a test without its tester, scope or report link, findings filed late, or a major release tag with no test, or only one dated after the tag. Tags created before the date in adopted: are not checked.\n\nTool: Node.js with js-yaml and git for-each-ref (scripts/security/pentest-record.mjs) in GitHub Actions\n\n```js\n// scripts/security/pentest-record.mjs; security/pentests.yaml looks like:\n//   adopted: 2026-11-01\n//   tests: [{ date: 2027-03-10, tester: Example Security Ltd, scope: Order API and Storefront, release: v3.0.0, report: https://..., findings_filed: 2027-03-14 }]\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process'\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst MAJOR = /{{MAJOR_TAGS}}/, TRIAGE_DAYS = {{PENTEST_TRIAGE_DAYS}}\nconst { adopted, tests = [] } = yaml.load(readFileSync('security/pentests.yaml', 'utf8'), { schema: yaml.JSON_SCHEMA })\nconst errors = []\nfor (const t of tests) {\n  if (!t.tester || !t.scope || !t.report) errors.push(`test of ${t.date}: tester, scope and report are required`)\n  if (!((new Date(t.findings_filed) - new Date(t.date)) / 86_400_000 <= TRIAGE_DAYS)) errors.push(`test of ${t.date}: findings not filed within ${TRIAGE_DAYS} days`)\n}\nconst tags = execFileSync('git', ['for-each-ref', 'refs/tags', '--format=%(refname:short) %(creatordate:short)'], { encoding: 'utf8' })\n  .split('\\n').filter(Boolean).map((line) => line.split(' '))\nfor (const [tag, date] of tags.filter(([tag, date]) => MAJOR.test(tag) && new Date(date) >= new Date(adopted))) {\n  const t = tests.find((x) => x.release === tag)\n  if (!t || new Date(t.date) > new Date(date)) errors.push(`${tag} (${date}): no penetration test before the release`)\n}\nif (errors.length) { console.error(errors.join('\\n')); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "PENTEST": [
          "yearly",
          "major"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-pentest-waiver",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "No-test record current",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1170,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "security/pentests.yaml holds a waiver with a reason and a replacement, reviewed within {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}} months",
      "description": "A scheduled job reads the waiver in security/pentests.yaml and fails when it has no reason, names nothing that replaces the test, or was last reviewed too long ago.\n\nTool: Node.js with js-yaml (scripts/security/pentest-waiver.mjs) in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```js\n// scripts/security/pentest-waiver.mjs; security/pentests.yaml looks like:\n//   waiver: { reason: internal tool, no personal data, replaced_by: [ZAP baseline scan per deploy (Secure coding)], reviewed: 2026-10-01 }\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst MONTHS = {{ASSESSMENT_MONTHS}}\nconst { waiver } = yaml.load(readFileSync('security/pentests.yaml', 'utf8'), { schema: yaml.JSON_SCHEMA })\nconst oldest = new Date(); oldest.setMonth(oldest.getMonth() - MONTHS)\nconst errors = []\nif (!waiver?.reason) errors.push('the waiver gives no reason')\nif (!waiver?.replaced_by?.length) errors.push('the waiver names nothing that replaces the test')\nif (!(new Date(waiver?.reviewed) >= oldest)) errors.push(`the waiver was last reviewed ${waiver?.reviewed}, more than ${MONTHS} months ago`)\nif (errors.length) { console.error(errors.join('\\n')); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "PENTEST": [
          "none"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-training-onboarding",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "New committers trained",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1300,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 people who committed in the last 90 days, first committed more than {{ONBOARDING_DAYS}} days ago, and have no completion of {{TRAINING_COURSE}}",
      "description": "A weekly job downloads the completions from the learning platform (its export or API, reduced to email,course,completed_on in training.csv and never committed), takes each author's first commit date from git log, and fails naming everyone who committed recently and is past the onboarding window without a completion. Commits must use the work address the learning platform knows; bots are skipped.\n\nTool: Node.js and git log (scripts/security/training-onboarding.mjs) in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```js\n// scripts/security/training-onboarding.mjs; training.csv: email,course,completed_on (downloaded by the job, never committed)\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process'\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\n\nconst COURSE = '{{TRAINING_COURSE}}', DAYS = {{ONBOARDING_DAYS}}\nconst trained = new Set(readFileSync('training.csv', 'utf8').trim().split('\\n').slice(1)\n  .map((l) => l.split(',')).filter(([, course]) => course === COURSE).map(([email]) => email.toLowerCase()))\nconst git = (...args) => execFileSync('git', ['log', '--no-merges', ...args], { encoding: 'utf8' }).split('\\n').filter(Boolean)\nconst first = new Map()\nfor (const line of git('--reverse', '--format=%ae %as')) {\n  const [email, date] = line.split(' ')\n  if (!first.has(email.toLowerCase())) first.set(email.toLowerCase(), new Date(date))\n}\nconst recent = new Set(git('--since=90 days ago', '--format=%ae').map((e) => e.toLowerCase()).filter((e) => !/\\[bot\\]/.test(e)))\nconst due = (email) => (Date.now() - first.get(email)) / 86_400_000 > DAYS\nconst missing = [...recent].filter((email) => due(email) && !trained.has(email))\nif (missing.length) { console.error(`no completion of ${COURSE}:\\n${missing.join('\\n')}`); process.exit(1) }\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-training-yearly",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Committers trained in the last 12 months",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1430,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 people who committed in the last 90 days without a completion of {{TRAINING_COURSE}} in the last 12 months",
      "description": "The same weekly job compares each recent author's latest completion of the course with today, and fails naming everyone whose latest completion is older than 12 months.\n\nTool: Node.js and git log (scripts/security/training-yearly.mjs) in a scheduled GitHub Actions job\n\n```js\n// scripts/security/training-yearly.mjs; training.csv: email,course,completed_on (downloaded by the job, never committed)\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process'\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\n\nconst COURSE = '{{TRAINING_COURSE}}'\nconst latest = new Map()\nfor (const [email, course, on] of readFileSync('training.csv', 'utf8').trim().split('\\n').slice(1).map((l) => l.split(','))) {\n  const key = email.toLowerCase()\n  if (course === COURSE && !(latest.get(key) > new Date(on))) latest.set(key, new Date(on))\n}\nconst year = new Date(); year.setFullYear(year.getFullYear() - 1)\nconst recent = new Set(execFileSync('git', ['log', '--no-merges', '--since=90 days ago', '--format=%ae'], { encoding: 'utf8' })\n  .split('\\n').filter((e) => e && !/\\[bot\\]/.test(e)).map((e) => e.toLowerCase()))\nconst lapsed = [...recent].filter((email) => !(latest.get(email) >= year))\nif (lapsed.length) { console.error(`${COURSE} not completed in the last 12 months:\\n${lapsed.join('\\n')}`); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "TRAINING": [
          "yearly"
        ]
      }
    }
